  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-2y=22 \\\\ 5x+8y=14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe variable $y$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1\\. Multiply the first equation by $4$ and obtain\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}12x-8y=88.\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Add the multiplied equation to the second equation to get\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$=102.$\n\n$\\,$\nHence, the solution to the system of equations is $($\n{{field-2}}$\\,,$\n{{field-3}}\n$).$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by $4$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,3x-2y=22\\,)\\,\\times (4) \\\\ 5x+8y=14\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}12x-8y=88 \\\\ 5x+8y=14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-\\cancel{8y} & =88 \\\\ 5x+\\cancel{8y} & =14 \\\\ (12x+5x) & =(88+14) \\\\ 17x & =102\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=6.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=6$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2y & =22 \\\\ 3(6)-2y & =22 \\\\ 18-2y & =22 \\\\ -2y & =4 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=6,\\,y=-2,$ which can be expressed as $(\\,6,-2\\,).$",
